// @run
// @log 1
// A STRUCT FIELD TYPED AS A UNION ARM, ON THE DECLARATIONS ALONE — the retired `@emit-error`
// pin of D734, now the positive case it always described (closed by D761). There is still no
// value of `Circle` or `GW` anywhere in this program: `collectS` codes every declared
// struct's fields before anything is emitted, so the whole defect fired on the `type` block
// and the empty body proves it.
//
// WHAT THE PIN SAID WAS MISSING: `nameIsStructDecl` excludes a variant by name ("their fields
// ride the variant tables, not the struct table"), so the code-15 rung declined and nothing
// else claimed the field. Its ablation was four one-line edits that ALL RAN — delete the
// union, give the field the UNION (`g: Shape`, code 16), make it an arm-element ARRAY
// (`g: Circle[]`, code 5), or declare a plain-struct layout twin — and that is exactly why
// the refusal was a lost classification: the field wants the arm's heap, and since D280 the
// arm's heap IS a struct row's (`uVarHeap[vi] = sHeapIdx[uVarSTwinAt(vi)]`). It is coded 15
// against `variantStructHeapTwinAt` now, over a row interned from the arm's own render where
// the module declares no twin.
//
// THE SENTENCE IT ALSO PINNED IS STILL GONE AND STILL WORTH THE NOTE. Before D734 all three
// refusal sites printed `only i32 / boolean / string / array struct fields are supported`,
// which was FALSE of the compiler it belonged to (f64 is code 17, i64 23, f32 24, a MAP field
// 19, a nested STRUCT field 15, a union-BOX field 16, and the five scalar-list backings
// 25/26/27/6/4). Narrowing a message is not a substitute for building the lowering — that
// row said so about itself, and this file is what it looks like when the lowering lands.
type Sq = { s: i32 }
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type Shape = Circle | Sq
type GW = { g: Circle }

function rd() { }
rd()
print(1)
